<Base lang="en" title="100 Small Things About Toronto"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>100 Small Things About Toronto.</h1><p>A published essay collection: one hundred small entries plus a set of Montreal notes, written with no outline — the recurring threads only became visible in hindsight. A few of the lighter selections are below.</p><div class="ep-layout"><div class="ep-tabs"><button class="ep-tab active" data-ep="0">Ep.24 Tiramisu Made of Gold</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="1">Ep.34 The Sandwich Longer Than My Arm</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="2">Ep.47 Turns Out There Is Such a Thing as a Free Lunch</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="3">Ep.20 My Taste Buds Are Saved!</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="4">Ep.2 The Strange Taxi</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="5">Ep.9 Ice Latte and Double Double</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="6">Ep.13 Maple Syrup and Pancake</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="7">Ep.17 The Glass Bottle of Milk from Metro</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="8">Ep.18 The Good Eats of Kensington Market</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="9">Ep.30 The Vaughan Mills Heist</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="10">Ep.50 A Surprise Bag of Leftovers?</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="11">Ep.51 Center Island and Those Contemplations</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="12">Ep.53 Uber Eats VS Doordash</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="13">Ep.54 The Time Machine on a Little Street</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="14">Ep.55 Thirty Bucks for This?!</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="15">Ep.56 Wait? Our School Has That?</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="16">Ep.57 The Vibe of the Distillery District</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="17">Ep.58 The Second-hand Bookstore on College Street</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="18">Ep.66 That Day's Double Rainbow</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="19">Ep.84 Tomato Bisque and a Smoked Salmon Sandwich</button></div><div class="ep-panels"><div class="ep-panel active" data-ep="0"><p>To be honest, I had passed through downtown Toronto so many times, but the first time I ever heard of a high-end supermarket like Eataly was from my landlady.</p><p>But what I never expected was that my first time stepping into one of Toronto's gourmet supermarkets would be after I had already graduated.</p><p>My landlady was a young woman, not much older than me, so I often called her 'sis.' Seeing me still struggling between Uber Eats and DoorDash every day after graduation, she finally couldn't take it anymore and said to me, "Come on, I'm taking you to a good place to show you the real Italy."</p><p>That "good place" was Eataly, located on Bloor Street, the luxury shopping strip.</p><p>The moment I walked through the giant glass doors, I was completely captivated by the scene before me. This wasn't a supermarket. It was clearly a huge, three-dimensional, living and breathing Italian food theme park!</p><p>The first floor was a coffee bar and dessert counter, the air filled with the rich aroma of espresso and baked goods. People stood chatting with small coffee cups in hand, and the display cases were filled with all sorts of pastries so exquisite they looked like works of art.</p><p>The second floor was another world entirely. Fresh cheeses and hams hung at dedicated counters, chefs in white uniforms were making fresh pasta on the spot, and next to them were shelves selling all kinds of oddly shaped olive oils, balsamic vinegars, and seasonings.</p><p>The place was bustling with energy and noise, and everyone's face was glowing with a love for food.</p><p>Following my landlady, I felt like a country bumpkin who had stumbled into high society, my eyes barely able to take it all in. Everything here was a completely different world from the budget supermarkets I went to as a student, the ones that prioritized large portions and full stomachs.</p><p>Finally, we stopped in front of the dessert cooler. Amidst all the gorgeous panna cottas and fruit tarts, I spotted it at a glance—the tiramisu.</p><p>It was in a small, transparent square box, lying quietly under the light of the cooler. You could clearly see the distinct layers of coffee-soaked ladyfingers and thick mascarpone cream, topped with a layer of cocoa powder as thick as velvet.</p><p>"That's the one!" the dessert-lover in me screamed internally.</p><p>I reached out, pulled open the cold glass door, and picked up the box of tiramisu. Just before I placed it in my shopping basket, I saw the price tag. $20.</p><p>Twenty dollars for a small box of tiramisu. That price was enough for a chicken and rice meal from the Kabab shop behind the school, a meal that could last me for two. My 'broke international student' DNA screamed a piercing alarm at that moment.</p><p>My landlady saw my hesitation and said with a smile, "Trust me, you won't regret it."</p><p>With the tragic heroism of someone deciding to 'splurge just for today,' I placed the box of tiramisu into the cart.</p><p>After paying, we found a small table to sit at. I took a small spoon and, ceremoniously, scooped up the first bite.</p><p>The moment the spoon sank in, I knew the money was well spent. It was incredibly soft, with almost no resistance. I brought it to my mouth.</p><p>In that instant, I felt my taste buds awaken. The rich, slightly bitter cocoa powder melted on the tip of my tongue first, followed by the cool, silky mascarpone cream with a faint hint of liqueur, perfectly sweet without being cloying. At the bottom were the ladyfingers, soaked in rich espresso, with a moist texture and a deep, coffee bitterness.</p><p>These flavors blended perfectly in my mouth, layered and progressive, complex yet harmonious.</p><p>I admit, it was truly delicious. So delicious that I felt every single cent of that $20 was worth it.</p><p>It was then that I understood. Graduation might not just mean starting to look for a job and pay rent. It also means you can finally, occasionally, willingly pay for something 'unnecessary' but beautiful.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="1"><p>After the immense shock of the medium-sized Coke at Wendy's, the next thing to inspire in me a profound reverence for the concept of "North American Sizing" was the legendary "footlong" sub from Subway.</p><p>That afternoon, Ivy and I had just been liberated from a sleep-inducing accounting class and were discussing what to have for dinner.</p><p>"Syna, I'll treat you to dinner today," Ivy said, suddenly pulling a stack of papers from her backpack and waving them in front of me like a nouveau riche tycoon who had just struck it rich.</p><p>I leaned in to look. It was a stack of beautifully printed coupons, colorfully adorned with the logos and discount information of various fast-food chains.</p><p>"Where did you get these?" I asked curiously. In my world, coupons were something you usually had to actively seek out or snag on an app.</p><p>"Oh, they just came in the mail," she replied nonchalantly, as if it were as common as receiving a utility bill.</p><p>But why had I, living in the dorm, never received these in my mailbox? I felt like I had missed out on a billion dollars.</p><p>"Look at this!" Ivy said, pulling one out like she was presenting a treasure. "Subway, buy one footlong, get one free!"</p><p>I knew Subway; we have it back home, and its main selling point is being healthy. It sounded good, and a buy-one-get-one-free deal was a godsend for a frugal international student.</p><p>"Let's go!" I was immediately swept away by the word "free" and happily agreed.</p><p>At the Subway near the university, a peculiar smell of baked bread and various vegetables hit me. Ivy and I stood in line. While I was still studying the dense options on the menu, Ivy had already skillfully begun her order.</p><p>"A footlong, on Italian herbs and cheese bread. Teriyaki chicken, toasted. Provolone cheese," she rattled off, and then the staff member's gaze turned to me.</p><p>My turn. I instantly fell into the same panic as when I first ordered coffee. Which bread? Which meat? Which cheese? And that long row of colorful vegetables and sauces behind them! This wasn't ordering food; it was clearly a pop quiz!</p><p>"The... the same as hers, please." Faced with immense pressure of choice, I shamefully gave up.</p><p>Then, I watched as the employee pulled out something that looked like a baguette from the bread cabinet and sliced it open down the middle with a long knife.</p><p>My heart skipped a beat.</p><p>A footlong. That's 30.48 centimeters! It was almost longer than my forearm! I stared blankly as the employee stuffed that giant loaf with chicken and vegetables as if building a wall, finally squeezing on several lines of sauce. By the time it was wrapped in paper, it had become a heavy, thick, veritable "submarine."</p><p>When Ivy and I each sat down with a "submarine," I fully understood the sense of absurdity I had felt at Wendy's at the beginning of the semester.</p><p>I needed both hands just to hold the sandwich, feeling like a hamster clutching a giant ear of corn. I opened my mouth as wide as I could but could only manage to bite off a small corner of its cross-section. Meanwhile, Ivy next to me had already effortlessly demolished a quarter of hers, eating with great satisfaction.</p><p>In that moment, I finally had an epiphany. In North America, the concept of a "single serving" was probably not what I understood it to be. The "serving for one" here was designed for a 200-pound man.</p><p>That meal, I tried my best and still only managed to eat half of my "arm-length sandwich." I took the other half back to the dorm, heated it in the microwave the next day, and had it for lunch.</p><p>From then on, whenever I saw a coupon that said "Free," I would shudder. Because you never know what a heavy, sweet burden is hidden behind that "free" offer.</p><p>(Excuse me ????)</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="2"><p>Besides being the dorm's gossip center, Karen was actually a hidden "cooking fanatic" just like me. But since her family is Hindu, her culinary habits were rich with South Asian flair.</p><p>At first, due to certain stereotypes, I kept my distance from all Indian food. I always thought of it as a mysterious dark cuisine made up of countless spices I couldn't name and mushy textures.</p><p>Until one time, Karen showed off her skills in the dorm kitchen. She made a curry with chickpeas, potatoes, and various spices, served with fragrant rice. I took a bite with the attitude of "I'll risk my life to accompany a friend," and was instantly conquered by the rich, layered flavors of the spices. It turned out that real Indian food was completely different from what I had imagined! From then on, I became a loyal fan of Karen's cooking.</p><p>"What's this?" One day, I received a screenshot of a poster from Karen on Instagram. The design was simple, with a few cartoon vegetables and the words "Community Kitchen: Learn to Cook Healthy Meals!"</p><p>"It's a free cooking class at school," Karen sent in a voice message, her tone filled with the excitement of discovering a treasure. "I asked around and heard that you can take the food you cook for free right on the spot!"</p><p>The word "free," like a bolt of lightning, struck me right in the heart. As a poor and greedy international student, what could be more attractive than "free food"?</p><p>So, on a sunny Wednesday afternoon, Karen and I, two "foodie partners in crime," cheerfully headed to the "free lunch" site in the basement cafeteria.</p><p>It was a large area cordoned off by square tables, already filled with students. There were blond, blue-eyed local students, as well as international students like us from all over the world. A young white guy in a tall chef's hat was smilingly handing out aprons and recipes to everyone.</p><p>Today's theme was—Vietnamese Spring Rolls.</p><p>Karen and I received our "battle supplies": translucent rice paper, fresh shrimp, thin rice noodles, and a large pile of refreshing-looking vegetables like lettuce, mint leaves, and shredded carrots.</p><p>The process was much more complicated than I had imagined. It wasn't just about chopping and cooking; it was more like a delicate craft class. The hardest part was handling the dry, brittle rice paper. The chef told us to soak it in warm water for a few seconds until it became soft and transparent, but not too soft, or it would stick together into an unsalvageable tragedy.</p><p>I carefully placed the first sheet of rice paper into the water, silently counting "one, two, three," then clumsily pulled it out. The result was either still hard on one side or already a mushy mess on the other. Karen, on the other hand, once again showed her "cooking fanatic" talent, handling every sheet of rice paper perfectly.</p><p>The kitchen was filled with exclamations and laughter in various languages. Someone would wail because their rice paper tore, while another would cheer for successfully rolling their first spring roll. It felt less like a class and more like a lively and challenging craft party.</p><p>When we carefully wrapped the cooked shrimp, rice noodles, and various shredded vegetables in the soft rice paper, a translucent spring roll, faintly revealing the colorful fillings inside, was born in our hands.</p><p>"Okay, everyone! Time to enjoy the meal you made!" the chef announced loudly.</p><p>Karen and I took our plate of incredibly professional-looking Vietnamese spring rolls, paired with a special peanut sauce, and found a seat by the window. I picked one up, dipped it in the sauce, and took the first bite. The rice paper was soft and chewy, the shrimp sweet, the vegetables refreshing, and the aroma of mint exploded in my mouth, all complemented by the rich peanut sauce…</p><p>It was so delicious!</p><p>I looked at the students coming and going outside the window, then at the substantial, delicious, and most importantly—completely free—lunch in my hands, my heart filled with a huge sense of satisfaction and happiness.</p><p>It was then that I understood that the saying my parents taught me since I was a child, "There's no such thing as a free lunch," didn't hold true at this magical Canadian university.</p><p>Here, there was not only a free lunch, but also free cooking lessons, and the priceless joy of sharing food with friends.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="3"><p>If universities back home are backed by vibrant snack streets, then North American universities can only be described as "barren."</p><p>When I was still back home, the back alleys and snack streets of my university were my source of joy. There was steaming hot Malatang, sizzling Teppanyaki, fragrant grilled cold noodles, and the ever-popular Takoyaki with its perpetual queue. Every small stall was a portal to a comforting world of deliciousness.</p><p>But after arriving in Toronto, I discovered that the "cuisine" around the university was basically monopolized by a few fast-food giants. Tim Hortons, Subway, Pizza Pizza... they were like tireless copy-paste templates, occupying every prime location.</p><p>Eating them once or twice felt new, but after a solid week, I felt like my taste buds were being completely formatted.</p><p>Just when I thought my university life was destined to be a cycle of burgers, sandwiches, and donuts, I discovered the inconspicuous little street behind the university.</p><p>There were no bright signs of chain stores, only a few small, slightly worn-down storefronts. And the ones that left the deepest impression on me were the tiny sushi shop and the Kabab place next to it.</p><p>The sushi shop was so small that it only had one counter and two or three tables. The owners were a quiet Chinese couple. They didn't talk much and always wore gentle smiles. The husband was in charge of making sushi behind the counter, while the wife took orders and packed them.</p><p>Their movements weren't fast, but every step was filled with care. The first time I went, I ordered a simple salmon sushi. The owner took out a whole piece of fish from the cooler and, with a long knife, deftly sliced off a few pieces. The texture of the fish glistened freshly under the light.</p><p>He placed the fish slice on the warm rice and, with a gentle squeeze, a perfect piece of sushi was born. I put it in my mouth, and the richness of the fish and the slight tartness of the rice melted together. In that moment, I felt healed.</p><p>Later, that little sushi shop became my secret spot for treating myself. Oh, and besides the regular sushi, their poke bowls were also exceptional. Even after I graduated and moved to an off-campus apartment, I would still often order takeout from them on Uber Eats. It was a familiar comfort etched into my taste buds.</p><p>The Kabab shop next door, however, had a completely different vibe. The owner was an enthusiastic Middle Eastern man, always wearing an apron stained with sauce, who would greet every student loudly in his heavily accented English.</p><p>His shop was always filled with a powerful, rich aroma of grilled meat and various spices. Giant skewers of meat sizzled on the grill, the fat dripping onto the charcoal and sending up puffs of white smoke.</p><p>His shop was a blessing for all hungry students because it had one defining characteristic: huge portions, and cheap.</p><p>My usual order was the Chicken with Rice. For under twenty dollars (the price has probably gone up by now), the owner would give you a giant container piled high with golden spiced rice, topped with a mountain of grilled chicken, and served with a large portion of salad and golden-brown naan bread.</p><p>The portion was so substantial it could satisfy any big guy. Usually, I could split one dinner into two meals; reheating it in the microwave the next day made for another hearty lunch.</p><p>And so, one quiet, one lively; one delicate, one hearty. These two small shops, like two silent guardians, held up a space for us students, surrounded by bland campus food and fast food, to breathe—a world filled with human warmth and authentic flavors.</p><p>They were the most delicious and the brightest stars on my barren campus food map.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="4"><p>One thing about Canada is that they don't skimp on the air conditioning, not on the planes and certainly not in the airports.</p><p>Having just arrived from a connecting flight in Vancouver, the moment I stepped through the terminal's automatic doors, I was hit by a wave of early September heat that made my whole body jolt. The flush of embarrassment from the stuck wheel incident hadn't even faded from my cheeks. Now, as the hot wind hit my face, it created a wretched sensation, like being caught between fire and ice.</p><p>I stared silently at the 'No Service' indicator on my phone. It took a few restarts, but finally, the 4G signal flickered back to life. Breathing a sigh of relief, I followed the "Taxi" signs and, after waiting for about ten minutes in a somewhat chaotic curbside line, it was finally my turn.</p><p>The driver was a South Asian man with dark skin and a scruffy beard. He wordlessly helped me stuff my two giant suitcases into the trunk, his movements efficient, though he never once looked at me.</p><p>The moment I pulled the car door open, I froze.</p><p>Perhaps because the pandemic wasn't truly over, a thick plexiglass divider, stretching nearly to the roof, separated the back seats from the front.</p><p>In the middle of the divider was a small, semi-circular cutout, just large enough for a credit card or cash to pass through. The entire surface was covered in fine scratches, which, in the dim light of the car's interior, looked like a frozen, shattered spiderweb.</p><p>As I got in and shut the door, I felt less like I was in a taxi and more like I was being locked in a moving, airtight, transparent cage.</p><p>Since the school term hadn't started yet, I couldn't get into my dorm despite our email exchanges with the school. Down on my luck, I had no choice but to book a hotel as a temporary residence hastily.</p><p>I gave the driver the hotel address. He just glanced at me in the rearview mirror, grunted an indistinct 'uh-huh,' and started the car.</p><p>The radio played music in a language I couldn't understand, its fast-paced melody and chaotic drum beats sounding like a string of mumbled incantations, making the small backseat space feel even more confined. The car quickly entered the highway. I had expected a swift journey, but after only a few minutes, our speed dropped, and eventually, we came to a complete stop in the endless stream of traffic.</p><p>I peered out the window to see a frozen river formed by countless red taillights.</p><p>Cars were packed bumper-to-bumper, blanketing my entire field of vision with no end in sight.</p><p>In the distance, the city skyline was slowly sharpening in the morning light. I could even see the iconic spire of the CN Tower, but it was like a mirage—distant, unreal, its poetry dissolved by the frustration of the traffic jam and the silence inside the car.</p><p>My first real look at Toronto wasn't a postcard view. It was a distant, blurry silhouette seen through a dirty window while stuck in traffic on Highway 407.</p><p>"Is the traffic in Toronto always this bad?" I finally asked my first question since landing.</p><p>But no one answered.</p><p>The driver seemed very patient, or perhaps just used to it. One of his hands rested on the steering wheel, while the other tapped lightly on his leg to the rhythm of the radio.</p><p>He just sat there quietly on the other side of the divider, more like a programmed automaton than a person one could talk to.</p><p>A few times, I wanted to ask how much longer we'd be stuck, but the words died on my lips as I looked at that cold, impassive barrier.</p><p>That sheet of plexiglass was more concrete, more direct than the glass wall at the airport. It didn't just separate the space; it seemed to seal off language, warmth, and any possibility of connection.</p><p>My second encounter with this city was this experience of being enclosed, isolated, and immobilized.</p><p>I don't know how much time passed before the traffic finally began to crawl forward. When the car at last exited the highway and turned onto the city streets, I felt as if I were waking from a long dream. Outside the window, rows of unfamiliar shop signs and red-brick buildings flashed by.</p><p>And I was just a silent passenger in a transparent cage, about to be delivered to some random corner of this vast city.</p><p>"Here we are."</p><p>Dazed, it took me a moment to realize we had arrived. But as I stared at the dilapidated bungalow before me, I couldn't for the life of me believe this was the supposed four-star hotel I'd seen online.</p><p>I passed a hundred-dollar bill through the small cutout to the driver.</p><p>The driver gave me a suspicious look. He then started fumbling for something, muttering to himself.</p><p>I watched as he pulled his wallet from the armrest, digging out change while murmuring:</p><p>"This should be right."</p><p>The driver handed me the change. After confirming the amount was correct, I nodded at him.</p><p>The driver expertly killed the engine, parked, opened the trunk, and took out my suitcases.</p><p>"Alright, have a great day."</p><p>The black sedan turned and vanished around the corner, leaving me alone to wrestle with my two large suitcases in the wind.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="5"><p>Of course, any international student living in Canada knows that if there's a national beverage chain, the answer is, and can only be, Tim Hortons.</p><p>In Toronto, the existence of Tim Hortons is as natural as air and water. It's ubiquitous. On every street corner, at every gas station, in the lobby of every university building, you can see that familiar red sign.</p><p>It's not the kind of trendy coffee shop you make a special trip to for a photo op; it's more like an old friend, always there, offering you an affordable cup of coffee and a cloyingly sweet donut.</p><p>The first time I walked into a Tim's, I was practically dragged in by Leia and Karen on either side. It was an early morning, and we were rushing to an 8:30 AM class.</p><p>"Come on, Syna, let's go get some coffee to keep us alive," Karen said.</p><p>While waiting in line, I was still debating whether to get a latte or an Americano. However, I soon discovered that the ordering process here seemed to follow a secret code I wasn't privy to.</p><p>A construction worker in front of us said crisply to the cashier, "A large Double-Double, please."</p><p>Without looking up, the cashier operated the coffee machine, the movements seamless.</p><p>The guy in a baseball jersey before him used the same lingo: "A medium Double-Double."</p><p>Double-Double? What was that? A special kind of coffee bean? A seasonal secret menu item? I felt like a civilian who had stumbled into a spy rendezvous, surrounded by people communicating in a code I couldn't understand.</p><p>It was my turn. Faced with this huge gap in my knowledge, I chickened out and ordered the only safe option I recognized:</p><p>"An Iced Latte, please."</p><p>The cashier glanced at me, and in that look, there seemed to be a subtle, almost imperceptible judgment reserved for an amateur.</p><p>I took my iced latte and found a table with Leia and Karen. Both of their cups had a small "DD" printed on them.</p><p>Leia was about to take a sip of her coffee. Hearing my question, she paused for a second, then laughed. "Oh, that's just double cream, double sugar."</p><p>"That's it?" I was stunned.</p><p>"Yeah," Karen added from the side. "If you want one cream and one sugar, you just say 'Regular.' If you don't want anything, you say 'Black.' It's super convenient."</p><p>I looked at the iced latte in my hand, then at the "double cream, double sugar" in their cups, and felt like a complete idiot. I had spent twice as much money on a perfectly ordinary iced latte, while they, with a simple piece of slang, had gotten the most authentic Canadian national coffee.</p><p>It was then that I understood. The essence of Tim Hortons wasn't about how good the coffee was, but about its "language system"—a system built for efficiency that was deeply integrated into the daily lives of Canadians.</p><p>It's not fancy, it's not sophisticated, but it's fast enough, simple enough, to give every Canadian rushing to work or school a direct dose of calories and comfort in the shortest possible time.</p><p>Later, I tried ordering a "Double-Double" myself. The taste, how to put it... was just straightforwardly sweet, with the aroma of creamer, having little to do with the actual bitterness of the coffee.</p><p>But every time I see that familiar red sign on a cold winter day or a drowsy morning, I remember that morning, and the mysterious "secret code" that gave me my first real taste of cultural difference—</p><p>"Double-Double."</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="6"><p>After Leia's drinking capacity, the next thing that filled me with a profound sense of awe for the species known as "roommate" was Anne's fanaticism for sweets.</p><p>Anne was from Calgary, a place famous for the Stampede and the Rocky Mountains. I had assumed that a girl from such a rugged and hearty province would have a similarly bold palate. But I was wrong. Anne's taste buds seemed to exist for one word and one word only: sweet.</p><p>One Saturday morning, I woke up unusually early and, on a whim, decided to make pancakes in the dorm's communal kitchen for brunch. I mixed the batter, ladled golden circles onto the skillet, and the kitchen instantly filled with the aroma of milk and eggs.</p><p>I stacked the finished pancakes, decorated them with a few slices of banana and strawberry, and was about to drizzle a little honey on top to enjoy a healthy and delicious brunch.</p><p>Just then, drawn by the aroma, Anne appeared at the kitchen doorway in her pink pyjamas.</p><p>"Oh, pancakes! Smells so good!" she said with a smile, walking over.</p><p>"Do you want to eat together?" I invited her enthusiastically.</p><p>"Of course!" she replied cheerfully. Then, she pulled open one of our shared cupboards.</p><p>I watched as she took out a giant, maple-leaf-shaped glass bottle. It was filled with a dark amber, viscous-looking liquid—the Canadian specialty, maple syrup.</p><p>"How can you have pancakes without maple syrup?" she said, in a tone that suggested my intention to eat them with only fruit was a grave insult to the very idea of pancakes.</p><p>Then, she proceeded to put on a breathtaking performance of a "maple syrup waterfall."</p><p>She unscrewed the cap and tilted the giant maple leaf bottle. The thick syrup, like an amber river, flowed slowly but endlessly onto the stack of innocent pancakes.</p><p>It started from the top, drowning the strawberries, flooding past the bananas, soaking through the gaps of every single layer, and finally pooling at the bottom of the plate into a sticky, glistening "lake of syrup."</p><p>I was completely dumbfounded. This wasn't "drizzling" syrup; this was "marinating" the pancakes in it.</p><p>"Come and try it, this is the most authentic way to eat it," she said, pushing the syrup-drenched plate in front of me, her face beaming with a pride that screamed, "Come, taste our national treasure!"</p><p>I carefully cut off a small piece with my fork. The pancake was so thoroughly saturated that it was still dripping syrup as I lifted it. I put it in my mouth.</p><p>An explosive, unapologetic wave of sweetness instantly conquered all my taste buds. It was a pure, undisguised sweetness so intense it made my teeth ache. I felt like my insulin was screaming an alarm.</p><p>I managed to swallow that small piece, feeling like I needed a whole glass of water to recover. Meanwhile, Anne, next to me, was eating with utter contentment, even using her fork to scrape up and eat the remaining syrup on the plate.</p><p>It was then that I understood. In Canada, maple syrup might not be a condiment, but a main course.</p><p>I looked at the blissful expression on Anne's face, then at the giant maple leaf bottle on the table, and suddenly felt that my roommates were all extraordinary people with "hidden talents."</p><p>Leia could drink Tequila like water, and Anne could eat maple syrup like porridge.</p><p>And me, an ordinary person who could only handle iced lattes and dared only to put fruit on my pancakes, living with them under the same roof was truly a life full of new "surprises" and "challenges" every day.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="7"><p>I've always loved things with a rich milky flavor. Whether it was White Rabbit candy or Hokkaido ice cream, that full-bodied, sweet taste of milk always brought me the most direct and simple kind of happiness.</p><p>But in North America, finding a glass of milk that actually "tasted like milk" had become a luxury. The milk in the supermarket coolers, sold in giant plastic jugs or cardboard cartons, always tasted faint, like it was watered down, lacking a soul.</p><p>I was always confused by the 1% and 2% numbers on the packaging. It was only after looking it up online that I had a revelation—it was the fat content. The higher the number, the higher the fat content, and the richer the milky taste. And the most common options on the shelves were all these "low-fat" or "reduced-fat" varieties.</p><p>Until one time, while wandering through the dairy section at a Metro supermarket, my eyes were drawn to a row of "art pieces" in the corner of the cooler.</p><p>It was milk in heavy glass bottles. The bottles had a retro, rounded shape, sealed with a small red plastic cap. Through the glass, you could even see a thick layer of pale yellow cream floating on top.</p><p>They stood there quietly, looking particularly elegant and distinct compared to the colorful plastic containers next to them.</p><p>I was immediately smitten.</p><p>I picked one up. It was heavy, with beads of condensation on the glass, the coldness spreading from my fingertips. I glanced at the price tag—it was nearly double the price of regular milk, and a small line of text next to it read: "Plus $2 Deposit."</p><p>But what attracted me more was the small number on the label: 3.8%. This was a fat content I had never seen on regular milk, belonging to the richest category.</p><p>Deposit again! But this time, as a "mature adult," I would no longer panic at the word. I just silently calculated in my head whether it was worth spending the extra money for this touch of nostalgia and that 3.8% richness.</p><p>My inner foodie and my broke-student logic waged a fierce, thirty-second war in my mind. In the end, the foodie won.</p><p>I placed the bottle in my shopping cart as if I were handling a precious treasure.</p><p>Back at the dorm, without even waiting for it to warm up, I eagerly tore off the plastic cap. I didn't drink straight from the bottle; instead, I ceremoniously took out a glass and poured the milk into it.</p><p>The milk poured out, its texture noticeably thicker than regular milk, clinging to the side of the glass and leaving a white trail.</p><p>I took a deep breath and took the first sip.</p><p>This was it! This was the taste!</p><p>An incredibly mellow and rich milky flavor instantly exploded in my mouth. There was no hint of any unpleasant odor, only a pure, slightly sweet milkiness. The texture was as smooth as silk, and after swallowing, a lingering aftertaste remained in my mouth for a long time.</p><p>In that moment, I felt like I wasn't drinking milk, but a taste of childhood, the most simple and authentic flavor that has been abandoned in the era of fast-moving consumer goods.</p><p>From then on, going to Metro once a week to buy a bottle of 3.8% glass-bottled milk became a small, ritualistic luxury in my student life.</p><p>Although it was a bit of a hassle to remember to bring the empty bottle back to get the deposit, every time I drank that rich milk, I felt that the trouble was nothing compared to the happiness it brought.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="8"><p>I think I'm the embodiment of a foodie. As the old saying goes, food is heaven, and I'd heard that Toronto has some of the best Chinese and various Asian cuisines in all of North America. As a gourmand, how could I possibly miss out?</p><p>After growing tired of the barren food scene around the university, I set my next exploration target on the legendary "treasure trove"—Kensington Market.</p><p>Kensington Market is unlike any other place in Toronto. There are no skyscrapers, no chain stores, only rows of colorful Victorian houses covered in bold graffiti.</p><p>The air is always filled with a complex, peculiar aroma—a mixture of coffee, spices, old books, and various street foods.</p><p>I was completely captivated on my first visit. This place is a paradise for an Asian stomach.</p><p>At the entrance of an inconspicuous little shop, I saw a familiar Cantonese sign. I peered closer and, my goodness, it was rice noodle rolls (Cheung Fun)!</p><p>I immediately ordered a shrimp rice noodle roll. When the server brought it over, cut into small pieces and drizzled with a special soy sauce, I couldn't wait to spear a piece with my fork. The smooth, tender texture, full of the aroma of rice, paired with the savory soy sauce and the springy shrimp, instantly reminded me of the streetside breakfast stalls back home.</p><p>After finishing the rice noodle rolls, I turned into another alley, drawn by the rich aroma of beef bone broth. It was a small Pho restaurant, always packed with people.</p><p>I ordered a classic Pho Dac Biet (a special combination). When the steaming bowl was served, I took a deep breath. The complex fragrance, simmered from star anise, cinnamon, and beef bones, instantly dispelled all my fatigue.</p><p>I pushed the bean sprouts and basil into the hot broth, squeezed in some lime juice, and added a spoonful of Sriracha. A sip of broth, a bite of noodles, a piece of meat—that warm and rich flavor soothed me all the way from my tongue to my stomach.</p><p>Of course, next door to the Pho place was what could only be described as the "savior of international students"—the Banh Mi shop. For just four or five dollars (the price has probably gone up by now), you could get a huge sandwich on a crispy baguette, stuffed with grilled pork, pâté, pickled daikon, cilantro, and chili peppers.</p><p>The taste—sour, sweet, spicy, salty, and fragrant—was a multi-layered explosion in my mouth. The value for money was criminally good.</p><p>After such a feast, how could I skip dessert? Tucked away at the other end of the market was a low-key Thai dessert stall, and its star was the unforgettable mango sticky rice.</p><p>Warm scoops of sticky rice, fresh from the pot, were bathed in fragrant coconut milk, soft and glutinous with a gentle sweetness. Beside it were freshly cut ripe mangoes, golden and aromatic. A final drizzle of slightly salty coconut cream added a soft contrast to the whole experience.</p><p>Every bite was a fusion of creamy and sweet, as if happiness itself had been made edible.</p><p>Walking out of Kensington Market, I was carrying my leftover Banh Mi, the sweet taste of mango sticky rice still lingering in my mouth. It was then that I truly understood why so many people call Toronto a food capital.</p><p>Its charm isn't in the high-end Michelin restaurants, but precisely in corners like Kensington Market, filled with a lively, down-to-earth atmosphere that embraces flavors from all over the world. This place was the promised land for a foodie like me.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="9"><p>Any international student living in North York (or near Vaughan) knows that if Toronto is the "newbie village" for students, then Vaughan Mills Outlet is our own personal "armory" and "supply depot."</p><p>In Toronto, prices are high and the tax rate is touching. Every time you spend money, it feels like you're cutting off a piece of your own flesh. But at Vaughan Mills, the scene changes completely. This isn't shopping; it's treasure hunting, it's a battle, an unspoken game between you and the major brands to see who can get the better deal.</p><p>The first time I was dragged to Vaughan Mills by my roommate Anne was on an unremarkable weekend. She said to me in a mysterious "I'll show you the world" tone, "I'm taking you to a good place."</p><p>As soon as I walked into the huge, circular mall, I was stunned by the bustling atmosphere. Everyone here was walking briskly, with sharp eyes, carrying bags of all sizes, their faces beaming with the joy of a fruitful hunt.</p><p>Our first target was the Nike factory store. Back home, a new pair of Nikes could easily cost over a thousand RMB. But here, I watched with my own eyes as a pair of classic Air Force 1s had a price tag that clearly read: $79.99. I rubbed my eyes and looked closer to make sure there wasn't a hidden decimal point after the "7." Eighty dollars, and Canadian dollars at that! I felt my sense of value being violently shaken. This wasn't buying shoes; this was practically a giveaway!</p><p>However, the truly "god-tier" moment happened when we were preparing for the coming winter. Toronto's winter is the kind that can make you freeze to the point of questioning your existence. Before I came, I had heard that a Canada Goose jacket was standard issue. But when I saw the price tag, often over a thousand dollars, my poor soul felt a deep sting.</p><p>Just when I thought I was destined to spend the winter wrapped in three layers of Uniqlo, Anne took me into an outdoor brand store. In the deepest part of the sales section hung a row of unremarkable-looking down jackets. I casually picked one up, looked at the tag, and it read: "Withstands extreme weather down to -40°C." Then I looked at the price tag: $199.99.</p><p>Two hundred dollars. To withstand minus forty degrees.</p><p>I don't know how many times cheaper it was than a Canada Goose, I only know that in that moment, it shone with a holy light in my eyes. This jacket didn't represent fashion or a brand; it represented survival, dignity, the hope of a poor international student to live on in a harsh foreign land!</p><p>Of course, the surprises at Vaughan Mills didn't end there. During a summer sale, I even managed to find a T-shirt for only $30 in the Lululemon discount section. Lululemon! The middle-class yoga dream that was usually far out of reach for mere mortals like me! Thirty dollars, the price of a bubble tea and a dessert, and I actually owned it!</p><p>It was late when we left Vaughan Mills. Anne and I were loaded down with our spoils. Though our wallets were empty, our hearts were filled with an unprecedented sense of abundance.</p><p>Those moments of "robbing money" weren't robbing my money, but the brands' "lives." Every successful deal felt like winning a small victory medal for myself in this survival game of student life abroad.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="10"><p>For a cyber surfer like me who frequently browses Bilibili and Rednote, the concept of a "leftover blind box" was nothing new.</p><p>The idea sounded eco-friendly, had the thrill of opening a blind box, and was especially perfect for a poor international student like me. Eating leftovers sounded exactly like something I would do. After all, who knew if one day we international students would end up homeless, digging through trash cans for scraps. This kind of tragic self-mockery was the initial motivation for me to download that dark green app.</p><p>The app is called Too Good To Go. Its icon is green, promoting the idea of not wasting food. As soon as I opened it, the map was densely marked with all the nearby restaurants and supermarkets participating in the program, like a treasure map waiting to be explored.</p><p>My inner foodie's radar activated instantly, and I quickly locked onto a highly-rated Italian supermarket. In the pictures, it looked bright and clean, filled with all sorts of fancy hams and cheeses I could never normally afford.</p><p>Like a gamer trying to snipe a limited-edition collectible, I refreshed the page at the exact right moment and finally managed to snag a "Surprise Bag" they had released.</p><p>The pickup time was in the evening. With the nervous excitement of meeting an online friend, I went to the supermarket. I walked up to the cashier, a bit shyly opened the app, and showed my order page to the staff.</p><p>The cashier was a handsome young Italian guy. He glanced at my phone, gave me a knowing smile, and then turned to a large fridge behind him, pulling out a heavy, bulging brown paper bag and handing it to me.</p><p>Taking the bag, I felt like a secret agent who had just completed a clandestine exchange.</p><p>Back at the dorm, I couldn't wait to dump my "spoils" onto the table. And then, I was rendered speechless by the sight before me.</p><p>I stared at the pile of food, not knowing whether to laugh or cry: two boxes of delicious-looking pasta with fresh tomato sauce and meatballs, and two ridiculously large Italian bread loaves that were bigger than my face. All of this, for only $12 after tax.</p><p>Was this a joke? I rubbed my eyes and poked the still-warm pasta container. No, it was all real.</p><p>The two boxes of pasta were enough for two hearty dinners. And the two giant loaves of bread, I figured I could eat them with my peanut butter for half a week.</p><p>In that moment, I felt like I hadn't just bought a surprise bag of leftovers; I had robbed an Italian supermarket. The joy of getting such a huge return for such a low price, that sense of triumph over "evil capitalism," was more satisfying than any fancy meal.</p><p>From then on, snagging "surprise bags" became a new, exciting, and surprising hobby in my life as an international student.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="11"><p>If I had to choose my favorite place from Toronto's collection of sometimes shabby, inefficient, and maddening spots, it would undoubtedly be Center Island.</p><p>It's like a gentle, hidden back door to this huge, bustling, and sometimes indifferent city—a place to catch your breath.</p><p>To get there, you have to take a slow ferry from the downtown terminal. I love that process. When the ferry sounds its horn and slowly pulls away from the harbor, you feel yourself physically and ceremoniously bidding farewell to the headaches of schoolwork, bills, and social pressures.</p><p>The city's clamor is left further and further behind, replaced by the wind off Lake Ontario and the cries of water birds skimming the surface.</p><p>During that ten-minute journey, Toronto's skyline becomes a constantly changing, magnificent backdrop. The skyscrapers that usually feel oppressive, piercing the clouds, now become silent, exquisite silhouettes.</p><p>Looking at them, you feel like a chess piece that has temporarily escaped the board, gaining a brief, god-like perspective.</p><p>The moment you step onto the island, it's as if the world's volume has been turned down. There are no car engines, no maddening TTC announcements, only the rustle of wind through the leaves and the distant laughter of children.</p><p>Vast, neatly trimmed lawns spread out like green velvet carpets, inviting every weary soul to lie down.</p><p>On summer evenings, this place becomes a "second cafeteria" for University of Toronto students. You'll see groups of young people sitting on picnic blankets, sharing pizza and beer, playing frisbee, and laughing loudly, the air thick with the hormones of youth.</p><p>Even though I was also a student in the prime of my life, I usually didn't join them. I would just watch from a distance, feeling like an audience member watching a coming-of-age film.</p><p>I always came alone.</p><p>I would find a spot under a large tree on an empty patch of grass and sit down, taking my iPad and Apple Pencil from my backpack. This was my secret studio, where I created a different, happy ending for the characters I wrote about, one that diverged from the official canon.</p><p>I'd open my notes app, and the character I was currently "saving" would appear on the screen. Perhaps in the original story, his fate was tragic; perhaps he sacrificed himself for some grand goal; or perhaps he was ultimately separated from his beloved. But with my pen, all of this could be rewritten.</p><p>I would immerse myself in that world, and time would seem to stand still. For the character covered in wounds, I'd use my pen to give him a warm new coat; for the lovers forced apart, I'd add a scene of them walking hand-in-hand in the sun; I'd modify their dialogue, adjust their expressions, and with every stroke, I'd smooth over the regrets left by the official story that I couldn't let go of.</p><p>It's a strange, almost god-like experience. In real life, I can't control my grades, predict my future, or even decide if it will blizzard tomorrow. But here, on this small, glowing screen, I am the only god. I can decide everyone's fate, and for every virtual soul I deeply love, I can create a gentle, hopeful ending.</p><p>This is perhaps the most luxurious comfort I can give them, and myself.</p><p>When I pull myself out of that virtual world, it is usually dusk. The sun begins to set, and the summer sunset, like an overturned paint palette, dyes the entire sky in a magnificent gradient from orange-red to pinkish-purple.</p><p>I'd pack up my iPad and walk to the island's shore, looking across the shimmering lake at Toronto on the other side.</p><p>The skyline, formed by the CN Tower and the countless financial office buildings on Bay Street, looked as beautiful as an unreal painting against the sunset. Every glass curtain wall reflected the afterglow, as if the entire city had been plated with a warm, golden edge. It looked so prosperous, so successful, so full of infinite possibilities.</p><p>Nearby, parents were playing by the water with their laughing children, their laughter as crisp as wind chimes. They were carefree, and their futures, like the glowing city before them, seemed full of hope.</p><p>And me?</p><p>I looked at that magnificent city silhouette and then thought about myself.</p><p>My grades were less than ideal; I always played the most inconspicuous role in group projects. My abilities were mediocre; I had neither Ivy's innate artistic talent nor Leia and Karen's fearless social skills. Even in the area I was best at, I was just a reclusive otaku immersed in my own world, making small edits for virtual characters.</p><p>To come to this city, I had spent all my parents' savings and carried their expectations. I studied frantically here, experienced all kinds of culture shock here, and clumsily learned how to be an independent adult here.</p><p>But then what?</p><p>Where was my future, really?</p><p>Could I graduate successfully? Could I find a decent job? Could I find a small place that was truly mine in this seemingly prosperous city? Or would I, in the end, just be a passing guest, returning to where I came from a few years later with a diploma and a pile of complicated memories, to start all over again?</p><p>These questions, like the mist rising from the lake at dusk, silently yet pervasively enveloped me. I felt that between me and the glittering city on the other shore lay an insurmountable, cold expanse of water.</p><p>It was so close I could see the outline of every building.</p><p>And yet it was so far, so far that I didn't know how to reach it.</p><p>I stood on the shore for a long time, until the last glimmer of the sunset was swallowed by the night, and the city lights came on one by one, like a cold, starry sky reflected on the ground.</p><p>I turned and walked towards the ferry terminal for the return trip.</p><p>Center Island didn't give me any answers. It just gave me a safe, undisturbed space to face these questions. It was like a gentle, silent friend, offering me a quiet sanctuary to escape to when I was suffocating under the weight of reality.</p><p>The ferry sounded its horn again, slowly sailing towards that vast city of countless lights, a city full of the unknown and challenges.</p><p>I knew that tomorrow, I would be back to dealing with those headaches of schoolwork and bills.</p><p>But at least for tonight, I had had the tranquility of an entire island, and a period of time that was all my own, where I could freely create happy endings.</p><p>And that was enough.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="12"><p>As everyone knows, I'm a foodie, to the point where I've even coined the bizarre motto: "If you're not enthusiastic about eating, there's something wrong with your brain."</p><p>As a devout believer in good food, in a place like Toronto where eating out isn't always convenient and cooking for yourself is a hassle, food delivery apps became my lifeline.</p><p>Just as China has the epic battle between Ele.me and Meituan, when the battlefield shifts to North America, it naturally becomes a rivalry between the two giants: Uber Eats and DoorDash. And I, a poor and greedy international student, was caught in the crossfire, staging a daily internal drama about "how to spend the least amount of money to eat the food I crave the most."</p><p>The battle usually played out like this:</p><p>On an unremarkable evening when I didn't feel like cooking, I'd be lying on my bed and would open my phone. I wanted a bubble tea, and I wanted a steaming hot bowl of pho.</p><p><strong>Round One: Uber Eats strikes first.</strong></p><p>I'd skillfully open the green app. As soon as I entered, the big "Buy One, Get One Free" promotion on the homepage would launch a fierce, sweet assault. So tempting! It meant I could get two bubble teas for the price of one! One for tonight, one for tomorrow—a perfect plan.</p><p>I cheerfully selected the roasted brown sugar bubble tea I wanted and added a bowl of pho. Looking at the full shopping cart, I felt a huge sense of satisfaction. Then, full of confidence, I tapped the "Go to Checkout" button.</p><p>The next second, my smile froze.</p><p>On the bill, besides the cost of the food, was a long list of exorbitant fees I didn't understand but had to pay: Delivery Fee, Service Fee, Taxes, and sometimes even a baffling Extra Tip.</p><p>After that combo punch, my "free" bubble tea felt like it had been forcibly taken back. The final price was high enough to make me question my life choices.</p><p><strong>Round Two: DoorDash's counter-attack.</strong></p><p>Unwilling to give up, I closed Uber Eats and immediately opened the orange app—DoorDash. Its killer move was usually "$0 Delivery Fee" or a very low delivery fee.</p><p>Sure enough, I quickly found a restaurant that had both pho and bubble tea, with "Free Delivery" prominently displayed. I breathed a sigh of relief, feeling like I had saved a fortune. I added the same items to my cart, and then, I noticed something was off.</p><p>The pho at this place was $1 more expensive than the one on Uber Eats; the bubble tea was also 50 cents more.</p><p>I understood instantly. There's no such thing as a free lunch, and no such thing as free delivery. The so-called "free delivery" was just the fee cleverly distributed into the menu prices. It was a more subtle, "gentler" way of ripping you off.</p><p><strong>Final Round: The battle within.</strong></p><p>So, on that hungry night, I wasn't enjoying food, but performing complex mathematical calculations.</p><p>I took out my calculator and compared the final prices from both apps, down to the last two decimal places. I had to calculate whether the "seemingly free but with a pile of extra fees" buy-one-get-one-free from Uber Eats was a better deal, or if the "seemingly free delivery but with higher menu prices" combo from DoorDash was more conscientious.</p><p>This was no longer just ordering takeout; it was an ultimate game of psychology, mathematics, and consumerism.</p><p>In the end, I would probably choose the platform that was a dollar and thirty cents cheaper in total, and then eat my dinner with great satisfaction.</p><p>In this war between Uber Eats and DoorDash, there are no permanent winners. Today, you might have the upper hand with a buy-one-get-one-free; tomorrow, they can win back the ground with free delivery. And I, I just keep jumping back and forth between the promotional traps of these two giants, enjoying every moment of it.</p><p>After all, for a qualified foodie, the joy of eating what you want for the least amount of money, that feeling of triumph over capitalism, is even more delicious than the food itself.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="13"><p>I have to say, the other side of Toronto's inefficiency might just be its retro charm.</p><p>In this digital age where everything pursues "speed," this city still stubbornly holds on to some slow, seemingly outdated things, like the clanging streetcars, the banks with a half-month queue, and, for instance, physical record stores.</p><p>The first time I learned about a record store like this downtown was during a boring general education class, when I overheard two trendy girls in front of me chatting about it.</p><p>So, I took a quiet Saturday afternoon, took the subway by myself, and went to this legendary record store.</p><p>It was hidden on an inconspicuous little street. The storefront wasn't large, but the moment I pushed open the door with its little bell, a treasure world filled with music and time opened up to me.</p><p>The air was filled with a warm scent unique to old paper and vinyl records. Rows of huge wooden shelves were crammed with all sorts of CDs and vinyls, from classical to rock, jazz to pop, a dazzling array.</p><p>Like a child who had stumbled into a candy house, I slowly walked through the narrow aisles, my fingertips brushing across the album covers.</p><p>In the most prominent spot, I saw the new colored vinyl from Taylor Swift. The beautiful, art-piece-like record shimmered under the light, reminding me that it was still 2024.</p><p>But when I turned into another aisle, my breath caught.</p><p>Amidst a pile of CDs from classic bands, I saw a logo that couldn't be more familiar—ABBA.</p><p>In disbelief, I pulled out a greatest hits collection. Looking at the four spirited Swedes on the cover, dressed in their glamorous 70s performance costumes, I felt like I had discovered a treasure forgotten by time. Back home, it's almost impossible to find a CD store anymore, let alone a physical album from an "ancient" band like ABBA.</p><p>I clutched the CD tightly in my hand, feeling like I wasn't holding a plastic disc, but a tangible, sparkling piece of music history.</p><p>It was then that I understood that Toronto's maddening "slowness" might also have its lovely side. It is precisely because of this slowness that some beautiful, old things have been carefully preserved.</p><p>In an era where everyone listens to music on Spotify, to have a place where you can find a CD from decades ago, or a brand-new colored vinyl, that feeling itself is a luxurious, retro kind of romance.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="14"><p>As a student without a car, relying solely on my own two feet and the TTC, the weekly grocery haul was a severe test of my physical endurance. Every time I came out of the supermarket, both my hands were full of heavy plastic bags, the handles digging deep into my fingers. The walk back to the dorm felt like a painful, loaded cross-country trek.</p><p>I finally couldn't take it anymore. I decided I needed a small trolley for groceries.</p><p>My first choice, naturally, was the one and only Canadian Tire—a name that sounds like it has everything.</p><p>The store was huge, like a warehouse. It sold everything from car tires and barbecue grills to pots, pans, and gardening tools. I wandered around like a headless fly for a long time before finally finding my coveted trolley in an inconspicuous corner.</p><p>It looked unremarkable—a simple metal frame with a black canvas bag. I flipped over the price tag.</p><p>$29.99.</p><p>Thirty bucks? For such a flimsy-looking little trolley? My "broke international student" DNA screamed an alarm once again. That price could get me two hearty Kabab meals.</p><p>But the thought of no longer having to endure the pain of my fingers being cut into by plastic bags made me grit my teeth and buy it.</p><p>Filled with the joy of an impending "major quality of life upgrade," I pulled my brand-new trolley along on the way back to the dorm. However, about halfway there, I suddenly heard a soft plop.</p><p>I looked down and saw a box of cereal I had just bought, lying upright on the sidewalk.</p><p>I froze. How... how did it get out?</p><p>Puzzled, I inspected the canvas bag. And then, at the bottom of the bag, I found a hole—not too big, not too small, but just large enough for a box of cereal to make a precision "prison break."</p><p>My mind went blank. I had just bought it! It hadn't even completed a single full mission, and it had already deserted its post?</p><p>A surge of anger shot through me. I put the surviving cereal box back in the bag (cupping the bottom with my hand) and, fuming, dragged the "maimed" trolley all the way back to Canadian Tire.</p><p>I was mentally prepared for a three-hundred-round battle with the staff. After all, back home, trying to return a used item usually involved a complex war of words.</p><p>I walked up to the customer service counter and handed over the trolley and the receipt.</p><p>"Hi, I'd like to return this. There's a hole at the bottom," I stated my case concisely.</p><p>The lady at the counter didn't even bother to inspect the hole. She just took the receipt, typed a few things on her computer, and then asked, "Cash or back to your card?"</p><p>"Back to my card, please."</p><p>"Okay, all done."</p><p>The entire process took less than a minute. It was so fast it felt unreal.</p><p>I walked out of Canadian Tire with the successful refund receipt, my heart filled with mixed feelings. On one hand, I was amazed by the "lightning speed" and "no-questions-asked" return policy; on the other, I was speechless at their appallingly poor product quality.</p><p>From then on, I basically never bought anything that needed to be load-bearing or durable from Canadian Tire again.</p><p>After all, I had no desire to re-live the "surprise" of picking up my cereal from the sidewalk.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="15"><p>Long before I came to Toronto, I had heard that our university's list of electives included some very "peculiar" options, like African drumming and color exploration courses. I thought at the time, this university is quite the Renaissance institution, teaching everything.</p><p>But I never would have imagined that the real "Renaissance" wasn't on the course schedule at all, but was hidden in the basement of the Student Center we passed by eight hundred times a day.</p><p>The discoverer of this earth-shattering secret was, naturally, our dorm's most well-informed and gossipy "intelligence center"—Karen.</p><p>That night, she burst into our common area like a gust of wind, her face glowing with the excitement of someone who had discovered a new continent. She said to us mysteriously, "Girls! Guess what I found!"</p><p>"What? The school cafeteria released new products?" I asked without looking up.</p><p>"No!" she waved her hands excitedly. "There is a huge arcade hidden under the Student Center in our school!"</p><p>Leia, Anne, and I looked at each other, our faces all showing the same disbelief. Under the Student Center? I did remember a staircase leading to the basement, but the fire door at the bottom was always closed. I couldn't imagine what was hidden behind it.</p><p>"Why would I lie to you!" Karen stomped her foot, seeing our disbelief. "I went to the parking garage to see my friend today and saw it with my own eyes! There was everything in there!"</p><p>And so, under her vivid and persuasive "kidnapping," the three of us, half-believing, followed her like a group of secret agents on an expedition, marching towards the Student Center.</p><p>We crossed the noisy food court on the first floor, went around a few students who were studying, and found an inconspicuous staircase leading to the basement. The stairwell was dimly lit, with only a few emergency lights on. The further down we went, the more we could hear a muffled, rhythmic music and the din of voices.</p><p>When we pushed open the heavy fire door in the basement, a vibrant, colorful new world, completely different from the academic atmosphere upstairs, instantly exploded before our eyes.</p><p>It really was a huge arcade!</p><p>Dazzling neon lights flickered in the dim space, and the air was filled with a peculiar mix of popcorn and electronic components. The loud music, the ding-ding-ding of the arcade machines, and the cheers and laughter of people all mingled into a wave of heat that washed over us.</p><p>The four of us were stunned, standing at the entrance like four country bumpkins who had stumbled onto a movie set.</p><p>This place was an entertainment paradise! Along one wall was a whole row of flashing dance machines, with a few skilled players stomping out dazzling moves. On the other side were several gleaming bowling lanes, with the crisp sound of pins being struck echoing from time to time.</p><p>Further in, there were several pool tables with green felt tops, where a few guys were bent over, aiming their shots with professional-looking stances. But what surprised me the most was in the quietest corner of the arcade, where there were actually a few chessboards. Two calm middle-aged men were playing chess, completely absorbed and unbothered by the surrounding noise.</p><p>Action and stillness, noise and contemplation, modern and classic—all coexisted so harmoniously in this secret underground space.</p><p>It was then that I truly felt that I had only seen the tip of the iceberg of my university. It was like a giant castle full of hidden rooms and secret passages. You never know what kind of surprising new world you'll discover behind the next inconspicuous door.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="16"><p>The first time I went to the Distillery District, it was with a new friend, Lin.</p><p>Lin was Chinese, a typical INFP—quiet, introverted, but with an incredibly rich inner world. And I, a non-stop ENFP, always found a strange, complementary harmony when I was with her. We just clicked.</p><p>Our first visit was on a sunny summer afternoon. We took the streetcar and got off on the east side of downtown. The moment I stepped into the area, I felt like I had passed through an invisible door, instantly traveling from modern Toronto to a retro, bygone era of red brick, railway tracks, and Victorian industrial architecture.</p><p>The feel of the place was a lot like the trendy commercial streets back home that were converted from old factory buildings. The ground was paved with uneven cobblestones, and tall red-brick buildings had been transformed into galleries, designer shops, and cafes. Giant distillery vats and rusted pipes stood silently on street corners like works of art, telling tales of the area's past.</p><p>Lin and I had no specific destination; we just walked slowly, side by side. As the ENFP, I was in charge of chattering away, exclaiming at everything around us. As the INFP, she was in charge of listening quietly, and then, in the pauses between my excitement, she would respond to all my noise with one or two precise comments.</p><p>The summer sun was beautiful, and we were drawn to a long line outside a chocolate shop. It turned out everyone was there for their famous gelato. We each got a mixed-flavor cup and found a bench to sit on. The gelato was incredibly creamy and smooth, full of nutty aroma, and perfectly sweet. We sat there, eating our gelato, watching the summer-clad people pass by, soaking in the lazy, pleasant afternoon atmosphere.</p><p>Our second visit was in the cold of early winter.</p><p>The Distillery District is at its most beautiful during the Christmas season. Every winter, it hosts a grand Christmas market. When we arrived this time, the entire district was lit up with warm, twinkling lights, and the air was filled with the scent of mulled wine and roasted nuts.</p><p>We went into that familiar chocolate shop again. This time, what tempted us most was the steaming hot cocoa. We each held a cup and found a seat by the window. The hot cocoa was made from real melted dark chocolate, incredibly smooth and rich, with a slightly bitter aftertaste. Every sip felt like a warm hug, chasing away the winter chill.</p><p>It was getting dark when we left the shop. In the center of the square stood a huge Christmas tree, covered in ornaments and lights, like a sparkling giant from a fairy tale. Next to it was a giant, heart-shaped light installation displaying a countdown to Christmas. Countless couples and families were gathered there, taking pictures and laughing, their faces glowing with happiness.</p><p>Lin and I stood under that giant Christmas tree, taking it all in. We didn't speak, but in that moment, I felt as if we had said everything.</p><p>If Kensington Market represents the chaotic, diverse, and lively side of Toronto, then the Distillery District represents its refined, retro, and "atmospheric" side.</p><p>It's not a place to come alone; that would feel a bit lonely. It's best suited for a quiet friend who understands you, to walk with slowly through the different seasons, sharing gelato in the summer, holding a cup of hot cocoa in the winter, and just immersing yourselves in its unique, gentle atmosphere, an atmosphere brewed by time itself.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="17"><p>Back home, the pseudo-literary youth in me loved to wander through all kinds of different bookstores. From Sisyphus to Zhongshuge, Eslite to Tsutaya, I knew almost every designer bookstore like the back of my hand. It wasn't that I didn't love to read (I actually do), but I simply appreciated the clean, inky scent of new books.</p><p>Before coming to North America, I was mostly dismissive of second-hand books. Come on, only brand new, untouched books could be considered real books, right? Those old books, thumbed through and underlined by others, always felt like they had lost their soul.</p><p>However, since coming to Canada and facing the outrageously high book prices, I had to bow to reality. A thin paperback novel could easily cost twenty or thirty dollars, not to mention those brick-thick textbooks. My "new book complex" was so fragile in the face of poverty.</p><p>Just as I was leaving that Japanese cafe, ready to take the streetcar home, I noticed it. Not far from the cafe, there was a small second-hand bookstore.</p><p>Seeing it for the first time, I was reminded of scenes from indie films, those hidden corners of the city waiting for the right person to push open the door. The black sign with gold letters read "Sellers & Newel," exuding a classical elegance. A Canadian flag was casually hung in the window, and below it, on a long table in front of the door, were piles of old books waiting to be browsed. It stood there quietly, squeezed between a dry cleaner and another shop, like a stubborn dream keeper, guarding the bygone days held within the paper.</p><p>I walked closer, and a faint, dry scent of old paper seemed to fill the air. Everything inside was filled with books. Light filtered through the glass window, landing gently on the yellowed pages, as if time itself had slowed down here. Outside, the traffic rushed by; inside was a quiet, abundant world of words.</p><p>I pushed the door open and walked in. A warm, dry smell—a mixture of old paper, dust, and wood—greeted me. The space wasn't large, but every inch was crammed with books. Shelves stretched from floor to ceiling, and the aisles were so narrow that only one person could pass through at a time. There was even a creaky wooden staircase leading to a basement, which seemed to hide another world.</p><p>Like an explorer, I slowly "swam" through the sea of books. I ran my fingertips over the worn spines, imagining who their previous owners were and what stories they had lived through.</p><p>Then, in an inconspicuous corner, I saw it.</p><p>It was a small paperback, its cover slightly faded. I pulled it out. The title was <em>Almost There: The Onward Journey of a Dublin Woman.</em> I flipped through it; it was a memoir about a woman's growth. Besides the slightly yellowed pages, the book wasn't much different from a new one. I turned to the back of the title page and saw the price—a number "3" lightly written in pencil.</p><p>Three dollars.</p><p>I could hardly believe my eyes. Three dollars, the price of a cup of coffee, and I could own the complete life story of a strange woman from Dublin.</p><p>In that moment, I suddenly made peace with my "new book complex."</p><p>I took the book to the counter, my heart filled with the joy of having found a treasure.</p><p>Walking out of the bookstore, I looked at the three-dollar used book in my hand and suddenly felt that it was more precious than any brand new book with its fresh ink smell. Because its value wasn't just in the story itself, but also in the afternoon I found it, and in the invisible traces of time left by its previous owner.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="18"><p>Besides my hometown's plum rain season, I never expected that tens of thousands of kilometers away in Canada, there would also be a kind of sticky, suffocating "plum rain" weather.</p><p>Summer is supposed to be Canada's most beautiful season. Generous sunshine, azure skies, even the breeze is refreshing. But sometimes, it transforms and becomes as clingy as the cold winter. On those days, the air is filled with moisture, the sky is perpetually grey, and the whole world feels like it's been covered with a damp, not-fully-wrung-out towel—oppressive and gloomy.</p><p>Then, one afternoon, a downpour comparable to a typhoon descended without warning. Huge raindrops smashed against the windowpanes with a loud patter, and the sky turned as dark as ink, as if the end of the world had arrived early.</p><p>After the storm, the sky was still overcast and hadn't cleared up. Feeling suffocated by the stuffy air in the dorm, I decided to go for a walk on campus.</p><p>The post-rain campus air carried a fresh scent of mixed grass and earth. Puddles dotted the ground, reflecting the heavy, lead-grey sky. I walked slowly by myself, my mood as gloomy as the weather.</p><p>Just as I was walking past the lawn in front of the library with my head down, I happened to look up, and then, I froze.</p><p>Against the oppressive, grey canvas of the distant sky, a huge, vividly colored rainbow had quietly appeared at some point.</p><p>It was so clear, so complete, like a giant, seven-colored arch spanning between two academic buildings. The bright red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet, like a miracle, suddenly cut through the gloomy world, so beautiful it was almost unbelievable.</p><p>I stood there, staring at it blankly, feeling my breath grow lighter.</p><p>Then, I discovered something even more amazing.</p><p>Next to that incredibly clear rainbow, there was a faint, secondary arc with its colors reversed, like a shy, pale shadow nestled quietly beside it.</p><p>It was a double rainbow.</p><p>I took out my phone, wanting to capture this rare sight. But no matter how I adjusted the angle, I couldn't fully capture its magnificence and the magical atmosphere. I decided to just put my phone away. But, not wanting to give up, I still raised my camera and took a photo that didn't quite seem to capture the mood.</p><p>I stopped thinking about my headache-inducing essays, and I stopped thinking about the unresolved uncertainties of the future. I just stood there, quietly, greedily, using my eyes to memorize this fleeting, miraculous moment.</p><p>I don't know if seeing a double rainbow truly means good luck.</p><p>But at least in that moment, I felt that it was a gentle, silent comfort, a gift from this city that was sometimes maddening, and at other times, full of surprises.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="19"><p>In this country, where high-calorie foods like fried and sweet things are everywhere, finding a healthy meal can sometimes feel like searching for an oasis in a desert.</p><p>But the oasis does exist.</p><p>In an inconspicuous corner of the university, there was a small cafe. It wasn't like Tim Hortons, which always had a long line, nor was it like Starbucks, filled with students discussing group projects. It was always quiet, with only a few small wooden tables, and the air was always filled with a warm aroma of coffee and toast.</p><p>This place became my own "secret canteen."</p><p>Whenever I had an 8:30 AM class and didn't have time for breakfast, I would sneak over here alone after it ended. I didn't even need to look at the menu to skillfully order my "usual": a bowl of hot tomato bisque and a smoked salmon sandwich.</p><p>The tomato bisque was made from fresh tomatoes, not the industrial tang of canned food. It had a rich texture, a balance of sweet and tangy, and was topped with a sprinkle of fragrant pine nuts and a little chopped basil. On a morning after an early class, when my head was still groggy, a sip of that warm, appetizing flavor could instantly awaken all my senses.</p><p>And the smoked salmon sandwich was the highlight of the meal. The bread was toasted whole wheat, with a nutty aroma, filled with several large slices of beautifully orange-pink smoked salmon, paired with refreshing cream cheese, a few capers, and fresh arugula. The savory saltiness of the salmon, the smoothness of the cream cheese, the tiny explosion of salty tartness from the capers, and the unique peppery flavor of the arugula all blended together perfectly.</p><p>I would always find a seat by the window and slowly eat, a spoonful of soup, a bite of sandwich.</p><p>Outside the window were students coming and going in a hurry, while inside was my own quiet time, a moment to breathe.</p><p>In that moment, I didn't need to think about complex accounting formulas or worry about the deadline for my next paper. I was simply enjoying a healthy, delicious meal that could comfort my "Chinese stomach."</p><p>This was perhaps the simplest, and also the most luxurious, kind of happiness in my life as an international student.</p></div></div></div><p><a href="/en/writing/">← Back to Writing</a></p></article></Base>

<script is:inline>
  document.querySelectorAll('.ep-layout').forEach((layout) => {
    const tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    const panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active'));
        tab.classList.add('active');
        layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]').classList.add('active');
      });
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .prose:has(.ep-layout) { max-width:1000px; }
  .ep-layout { margin-top:24px; display:grid; grid-template-columns:230px 1fr; gap:36px; align-items:start; }
  .ep-tabs { display:flex; flex-direction:column; position:sticky; top:24px; }
  .ep-tab { text-align:left; background:none; border:none; border-left:2px solid var(--line); padding:11px 16px; font:inherit; font-size:.93rem; color:var(--muted); cursor:pointer; line-height:1.4; transition:color .18s ease,border-color .18s ease; }
  .ep-tab:hover { color:var(--ink); }
  .ep-tab.active { border-left-color:var(--rose); color:var(--ink); font-weight:500; }
  .ep-panels { min-width:0; }
  .ep-panel { display:none; gap:12px; max-width:100%; }
  .ep-panel.active { display:grid; }
  .ep-panel p { margin:0; color:var(--muted); font-size:.96rem; line-height:1.8; }
  @media (max-width:760px) { .ep-layout { grid-template-columns:1fr; } .ep-tabs { flex-direction:row; flex-wrap:wrap; position:static; gap:4px 14px; } .ep-tab { border-left:none; border-bottom:2px solid var(--line); padding:6px 2px; } .ep-tab.active { border-bottom-color:var(--rose); } }
</style>
